## Results

Both reports say `status=loaded`, `acceptance=counted`, and `guest_started=true`.
Both reached `retired=2,999,999,998` and `budget_exhausted`; both exact nonce
markers are present. CPL3 retired `873` instructions in each; the syscall
journal is identical (`9` entered, `8` returned). PM reads (`230`), PIT `0x61`
reads (`1`) and PIT `0x42` reads (`16,672`) also match.

| Run | RDTSC reads (all CPL0) | Delivered #PF | IRQ0 edges / delivered | Quanta | Halt idle ns | Virtual ns | Residual ns |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `g` | 34,454,856 | 33 | 34,811 / 34,816 | 34,581,308 | 1,304,120 | 37,473,063,118 | 34,471,529,000 |
| `h` | 34,446,881 | 34 | 34,803 / 34,808 | 34,572,928 | 1,579,898 | 37,465,363,896 | 34,463,554,000 |

In both runs, the sum of all retained site counts equals the runner's CPL0
RDTSC total; there are `22` sites, no unattributed reads, and no site overflow.
The largest site is `0xffffffff8271b34d` (`delay_tsc+0x4d`): 34,062,756 reads
in `g`, 34,054,713 in `h` (delta `-8,043`). The residual virtual-time delta is
exactly the total RDTSC/PIT read delta (`-7,975,000 ns`); full `virtual_ns`
differs by `-7,699,222 ns` because `h` has 275,778 ns more halt idle.

Changed RDTSC-site counts (`h - g`):

| RIP | Matched kernel symbol | g | h | Delta |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| `0xffffffff8271b34d` | `delay_tsc+0x4d` | 34,062,756 | 34,054,713 | -8,043 |
| `0xffffffff8200796e` | `extract_entropy.constprop.0+0xee` | 122,020 | 122,148 | +128 |
| `0xffffffff8184f2f1` | `__refill_objects_any+0x31` | 964 | 969 | +5 |
| `0xffffffff8271b31b` | `delay_tsc+0x1b` | 43,940 | 43,932 | -8 |
| `0xffffffff8200713e` | `add_interrupt_randomness+0xe` | 34,816 | 34,808 | -8 |
| `0xffffffff82726060` | `native_sched_clock+0x20` | 40,106 | 40,082 | -24 |
| `0xffffffff813afa20` | `read_tsc` | 138,054 | 138,029 | -25 |

The `delay_tsc` mappings were checked against this kernel's `vmlinux-debug` with
`nm -n` and `objdump -d`. The `+0x4d` instruction is `RDTSC` in a loop that
compares elapsed TSC against the requested delay and branches back while the
target has not elapsed. This localizes most count variation to the kernel delay
loop, but no call-stack attribution was captured; the data do not establish
whether it comes from call count, per-call requested delays, interrupt timing,
or another execution context. The resulting machine observation is not
classified as benign.

Each sidecar captured every delivered page fault (`33/33` and `34/34`, zero
omitted). Their event multisets differ by one additional event in `h`: CPL0
write at RIP `0xffffffff8271a9c1`, CR2 `0x7ffc659bc779`, error code `0x00000002`.
`nm -n` maps the RIP to `rep_movs_alternative+0x11`; `objdump -d` shows a
single-byte store through RDI at that instruction. The two runs' other page
fault events match in sequence: the first 30 records are identical, `h` has
this one additional event at zero-based index 30, and after removing it the
remaining event sequence exactly matches `g`. The insertion occurs immediately
before the same CPL3 instruction-fetch fault (`RIP=CR2=0x4000b0`) in both runs.
This narrows the difference to one successfully delivered write fault; it does
not explain why this access faulted only in `h` or establish that it is benign.

UART still differs in strict order: in `g`, the exact nonce line precedes
`Run /init as init process`; in `h`, the printk precedes the nonce line. The
terminal panic traces also differ: both contain `schedule`, `do_nanosleep` and
`hrtimer_nanosleep`, while `g` additionally contains optional frames such as
`__task_pid_nr_ns`, `handle_pte_fault`, `count_memcg_events`, `handle_mm_fault`
and `irqentry_exit` that are absent in `h`. The audit line remains after the
five thermal governor lines in both runs. No comparator was run for these single reports, no
normalization was changed, and S5-07 remains blocked/unaccepted; the ordered
UART and other machine differences are not declared benign.

